2. Is 'integer thresholds only in unit cells' EQUIVALENT to 'no non-trivial positive cycle'?
   No.  It is IMPLIED by it; the converse needs the cycle's word, read from its minimum, to be
   a dropping word, and that can fail.  Demonstration in the 3x+c family, where cycles exist:
==============================================================================
   c   cycle min   cycle cell   class dropping word cell   its threshold   word of cycle = dropping word?
    5       1      (3,1)      (2,1)                 5               False
    5      19      (5,3)      (5,3)                 19              True
    5       5      (2,1)      (2,1)                 5               True
    5      23      (5,3)      (5,3)                 23              True
    5     187      (27,17)    (27,17)               187             True
    5     347      (27,17)    (27,17)               347             True
    7       5      (4,2)      (4,2)                 5               True
    7       7      (2,1)      (2,1)                 7               True
   11       1      (6,2)      (4,2)                 55/7            False
   11      13      (14,8)     (13,8)                127699/1631     False
   11      11      (2,1)      (2,1)                 11              True
   13       1      (4,1)      (2,1)                 13              False
   13      13      (2,1)      (2,1)                 13              True
   13     131      (24,15)    (24,15)               131             True
   13     211      (8,5)      (8,5)                 211             True
   13     259      (8,5)      (8,5)                 259             True
   13     227      (8,5)      (8,5)                 227             True
   13     287      (8,5)      (8,5)                 287             True
   13     251      (8,5)      (8,5)                 251             True
   13     283      (8,5)      (8,5)                 283             True
   13     319      (8,5)      (8,5)                 319             True
   17       1      (7,2)      (2,1)                 17              False
   17      23      (31,18)    (10,6)                14977/295       False
   17      17      (2,1)      (2,1)                 17              True
   23      41      (43,26)    (34,21)               412939108693/6719515981  False
   23       5      (5,2)      (4,2)                 115/7           False
   23       7      (5,2)      (2,1)                 23              False
   23      23      (2,1)      (2,1)                 23              True
   29       1      (5,1)      (2,1)                 29              False
   29      11      (17,9)     (13,8)                329237/1631     False
   29      29      (2,1)      (2,1)                 29              True
   29    3811      (65,41)    (65,41)               3811            True
   11 of 32 positive cycles have a minimum that sits BELOW a non-integer (or
   integer but different) threshold of a SHORTER dropping word; their own word is not a dropping
   word, so 'no integer threshold' would not detect them.  E.g. 3x+5: the cycle {1,4,2} has word
   100, but the class 1 mod 4 has dropping word 10 with threshold 5; n = 1 is simply below it.
   The statement that IS equivalent in spirit is Terras' coefficient-stopping-time conjecture
   (every n >= 2 exceeds the threshold of its class); it implies 'no non-trivial cycle', and the
   converse implication is not known.

==============================================================================
3. Mirror and cousin: thresholds for 3x-1 and 5x+1 (positive integers)
==============================================================================
  3x-1: thresholds are -C(w)/D <= 0: every positive member of every dropping class drops at its
        coefficient stopping time; integer thresholds (k <= 20): [((1, 0), 0), ((2, 1), -1)]
        A positive integer fails to drop under 3x-1 iff its word never acquires a dropping prefix;
        the known ones are the cycle minima 1, 5, 17 (cells with D < 0).
  5x+1: dropping cells (k <= 16): [((1, 0), 1), ((3, 1), 1), ((5, 2), 2), ((7, 3), 5), ((10, 4), 14), ((12, 5), 56), ((14, 6), 202)]
        integer thresholds: [((1, 0), '0', 0), ((5, 2), '11000', 1), ((7, 3), '1110000', 13), ((7, 3), '1100100', 17)]
        (1,0) threshold 0 is the only D = +1 unit cell of 5x+1; the other integer thresholds are its
        'accident' cycles through 1 (cell (5,2), D = 7) and through 13, 17 (cell (7,3), D = 3).
